# Papuma.Kernel Sample — the shop walkthrough as httpYAC requests.
#
# Usage: start the sample (dotnet run --project samples/shop-minimal-api),
# then run the requests top to bottom (httpYAC: "send all" works — named
# responses chain the ids automatically; @sleep gives the feed handlers a moment).
# Environment: requests/http-client.env.json (host = http://localhost:5099).

### 1. Create a product with stock 5 — three writes, ONE atomic commit
# (product + inventory + StockReplenished fact share a correlationId)
# @name createProduct
POST {{host}}/products
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Espresso Machine",
  "price": 100,
  "initialStock": 5
}

### 2. A small order (total 100 ≤ 500) auto-approves; stock 5 → 4
# @name smallOrder
POST {{host}}/orders
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "productId": "{{createProduct.id}}",
  "quantity": 1,
  "customerEmail": "alice@example.com"
}

### 3. Oversell attempt → typed 409, stock untouched (the bounded counter, concepts §17)
POST {{host}}/orders
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "productId": "{{createProduct.id}}",
  "quantity": 99,
  "customerEmail": "greedy@example.com"
}

### 4. Check the stock: must be 4 (small order deducted, oversell rejected)
GET {{host}}/products/{{createProduct.id}}/stock

### 5. Replenish — Increment needs no expectedVersion; the delivery is a fact (event log)
POST {{host}}/products/{{createProduct.id}}/replenish
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "quantity": 10
}

### 6. An expensive order (total 600 > 500) → status "PendingApproval";
# the workflow handler materializes ApprovalTask "approval-<orderId>" (concepts §18)
# @name bigOrder
POST {{host}}/orders
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "productId": "{{createProduct.id}}",
  "quantity": 6,
  "customerEmail": "bob@example.com"
}

### 7. Approve — a normal write with expectedVersion. Run within 2 minutes,
# otherwise the escalation poller flips the task to "Escalated" first (the dueAt timer).
# @sleep 1500
POST {{host}}/approvals/approval-{{bigOrder.id}}/decide
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "decision": "Approved",
  "decidedBy": "manager@example.com"
}

### 8. Decide again → 409 (two approvers serialize typed, ADR-003)
POST {{host}}/approvals/approval-{{bigOrder.id}}/decide
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "decision": "Rejected",
  "decidedBy": "second-manager@example.com"
}

### 9. The order is now Approved (the workflow handler patched it)
# @sleep 1500
GET {{host}}/orders/{{bigOrder.id}}

### 10. The history IS the workflow execution log: v1 Insert (actor=bob),
# v2 Update PendingApproval→Approved (actor=manager) — and customerEmail
# appears only as {"changed": true}: the privacy policy at work (ADR-007).
GET {{host}}/orders/{{bigOrder.id}}/history

### ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
### Rejection flow: the saga compensation gives the stock back (concepts §18)

### 11. Another expensive order → PendingApproval, stock reserved
# @name rejectOrder
POST {{host}}/orders
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "productId": "{{createProduct.id}}",
  "quantity": 7,
  "customerEmail": "eve@example.com"
}

### 12. Note the stock right after placing (reserved)
GET {{host}}/products/{{createProduct.id}}/stock

### 13. Reject the approval task
# @sleep 1500
POST {{host}}/approvals/approval-{{rejectOrder.id}}/decide
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "decision": "Rejected",
  "decidedBy": "manager@example.com"
}

### 14. Order is Rejected …
# @sleep 1500
GET {{host}}/orders/{{rejectOrder.id}}

### 15. … and the stock came back (Increment(+quantity) compensation,
# atomic with the order transition in one handler session)
GET {{host}}/products/{{createProduct.id}}/stock

### ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
### Escalation (the dueAt timer): place an order needing approval and simply
### wait > 2 minutes without deciding — the poller patches the task to
### "Escalated" (visible in the task history; deciding afterwards still works).
